### Step 4 — Understand container lifecycle

```bash
# List running containers
docker ps

# List ALL containers including stopped
docker ps -a

# Stop a container (sends SIGTERM, waits, then SIGKILL)
docker stop nexaops-nginx

# Start it again
docker start nexaops-nginx

# Pause a container (freezes the process)
docker pause nexaops-nginx

# Unpause
docker unpause nexaops-nginx

# Execute a command in a running container
docker exec -it nexaops-nginx sh
# Inside: ls /etc/nginx/  then exit

# Remove a container (must be stopped first)
docker stop nexaops-nginx
docker rm nexaops-nginx

# Force remove a running container
docker rm -f nexaops-nginx

# Verify it is gone
docker ps -a
```

---

### Step 5 — Prove containers are ephemeral

```bash
# Run a container and create a file inside it
docker run -it --name ephemeral-test ubuntu:22.04 bash
# Inside the container:
echo "This data will disappear" > /tmp/nexaops-data.txt
cat /tmp/nexaops-data.txt
exit

# The container is stopped but still exists
docker ps -a | grep ephemeral-test

# Remove it
docker rm ephemeral-test

# Run a new container — the file is gone
docker run -it ubuntu:22.04 bash
cat /tmp/nexaops-data.txt  # No such file or directory
exit
docker rm $(docker ps -aq --filter ancestor=ubuntu:22.04)
```

This is the core principle: **containers are ephemeral**. Any data written inside a container is lost when the container is removed. Volumes (Task 3) solve this for persistent data.

