### ✅ Task 1 — Answers

**Image vs container?**
An image is a read-only template — a packaged snapshot of a filesystem and its metadata (OS, app code, dependencies, config). A container is a running instance of an image — it adds a thin writable layer on top of the image layers. You can run many containers from the same image simultaneously. The image never changes; the container's writable layer is temporary.

**What is the Docker daemon?**
A background service (`dockerd`) that does all the real work — managing images, starting/stopping containers, handling networking, and managing volumes. The `docker` CLI you type commands into is just a client that sends API requests to the daemon over a Unix socket (`/var/run/docker.sock`). The daemon can run on a remote machine — the CLI just needs the socket address.

**What happens when you run `docker run ubuntu`?**
Docker checks if the `ubuntu` image exists locally. If not, it pulls it from Docker Hub. It creates a new container from the image, starts the process specified in the image's CMD instruction, and attaches your terminal if `-it` was specified. When the process exits, the container stops.

**What is a container registry?**
A server that stores and distributes Docker images. Docker Hub is the default public registry. Others include AWS ECR, GitHub Container Registry (ghcr.io), and Google Artifact Registry. You push images to a registry so other machines can pull them.

**What does ephemeral mean for containers?**
Containers are designed to be temporary. Any data written inside a container's filesystem is lost when the container is removed. This is intentional — it forces a clean-slate approach and prevents configuration drift. Persistent data belongs in volumes, not in containers.

