### ✅ Task 2 — Answers

**How does each Dockerfile instruction create a layer?**
Every `RUN`, `COPY`, and `ADD` instruction creates a new read-only layer on top of the previous ones. Layers are cached — if an instruction and everything before it has not changed, Docker uses the cached layer instead of re-executing it. This is why you copy `requirements.txt` and run `pip install` before copying your app code — app code changes frequently, dependencies do not, so the pip install cache is preserved.

**What is a multi-stage build?**
A Dockerfile with multiple `FROM` statements. Each `FROM` starts a new stage with a fresh filesystem. You can copy specific files from earlier stages using `COPY --from=stage-name`. This lets you use build tools (gcc, npm, Maven) in early stages and produce a clean final image without them — dramatically reducing image size and attack surface.

**Why not run as root?**
If a vulnerability in the app allows remote code execution, the attacker gets root inside the container. Depending on the container configuration, this can be leveraged to escape to the host. A non-root user limits the blast radius — the attacker can only do what that user can do.

**What is `.dockerignore`?**
A file that tells Docker what NOT to include when it copies your build context (the directory you pass to `docker build`). Without it, `COPY . .` copies your `.git` folder, Python `__pycache__`, `.env` files, and everything else — bloating the image and potentially leaking secrets.

**`CMD` vs `ENTRYPOINT`?**
`ENTRYPOINT` sets the executable that always runs — it cannot be overridden without `--entrypoint`. `CMD` provides default arguments to the entrypoint — it CAN be overridden by arguments after the image name in `docker run`. Used together: `ENTRYPOINT ["gunicorn"]` + `CMD ["--workers", "2", "app:app"]` means `docker run myimage --workers 4 app:app` overrides the worker count but keeps gunicorn.

