### ✅ Task 3 — Answers

**What is a Docker bridge network?**
A private internal network created by Docker on the host. Containers attached to the same bridge network can communicate with each other using container names as hostnames. The bridge is isolated from the host network — external traffic can only reach containers via published ports (`-p`). The default bridge network does not support DNS by name — only custom bridge networks do.

**How do containers find each other on a network?**
Docker runs an embedded DNS server. On a custom bridge network, every container's name and any `--network-alias` values are registered in DNS. When `nexaops-dashboard` resolves `redis`, Docker DNS returns the IP of the `nexaops-redis` container. This is why you use `REDIS_HOST=redis` — not an IP address which could change.

**Bind mount vs named volume?**
A bind mount maps a specific host filesystem path into the container — great for development because you see code changes immediately. A named volume is managed entirely by Docker — the data is stored in Docker's storage area (`/var/lib/docker/volumes/`) and persists independently of any container. Named volumes are for production data; bind mounts are for development workflows.

**What happens to data when a container is removed?**
Anything written inside the container's writable layer is permanently gone when the container is removed. Data in a bind mount survives — it is on the host filesystem. Data in a named volume survives — Docker keeps it until you explicitly delete the volume with `docker volume rm`. This is why databases must always use volumes.

