### ✅ Task 4 — Answers

**What problem does Compose solve?**
Managing multiple containers manually is error-prone — wrong startup order, forgotten environment variables, missing network flags. Compose defines the entire stack as code in one file. `docker compose up -d` brings everything up correctly every time. It is self-documenting — the file shows exactly how the stack is configured.

**Service vs container?**
A service in Compose is the definition — the blueprint describing what image to use, what environment variables to set, what ports to expose. A container is the running instance of that service. One service can run multiple container instances (scaling).

**What does `depends_on` do and NOT do?**
`depends_on` controls startup order — Compose starts dependent services first. With `condition: service_healthy`, it waits until the health check passes before starting the dependent service. It does NOT wait for the application inside the container to be fully ready — only for the container to be running (without the condition). Always use health checks with `depends_on` for database and cache services.

**`docker compose up` vs `docker compose up --build`?**
Without `--build`, Compose uses the existing image if it exists — even if your Dockerfile changed. With `--build`, Compose rebuilds the image from the Dockerfile before starting. Always use `--build` after changing the Dockerfile or dependencies.

