## 15. Troubleshooting

**`Got permission denied while trying to connect to the Docker daemon`**
Your user is not in the docker group. Run `sudo usermod -aG docker $USER` then `newgrp docker` or log out and back in. Verify with `groups`.

**`docker compose up` exits immediately with `unhealthy`**
One of your services failed its health check. Run `docker compose logs postgres` (or whichever service) to see the error. Common cause: PostgreSQL not fully initialised before the health check fires — increase `start_period` in the health check config.

**`port is already allocated` when starting a container**
Something else is using that port on your host. Find it: `sudo netstat -tlnp | grep 5000`. Either stop that process or change the host port mapping in docker-compose.yml.

**Container starts but app is unreachable**
Check in this order: (1) `docker ps` — is the container running? (2) `docker logs container` — any error on startup? (3) is the port published? (`docker inspect container | grep Ports`) (4) security group — is the port open in AWS?

**`docker: Error response from daemon: pull access denied`**
The image is private or does not exist. Check the image name and tag. If it is yours on Docker Hub, run `docker login` first.

**Flask app starts but shows "Service Unavailable" for Redis/PostgreSQL**
The app starts without Redis/PostgreSQL — it falls back to default data. Verify both containers are running on the same network: `docker network inspect nexaops-network`. The dashboard should show Redis and PostgreSQL as "connected" in the `/health` response.

**`exec format error` when running on EC2**
You built the image on an ARM Mac (Apple Silicon) and are running on x86 EC2. Build for the correct platform: `docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 -t image:tag .`

**`docker compose down -v` deleted my database**
The `-v` flag removes all volumes. For production, never use `-v`. To preserve data: `docker compose down` (no `-v`), back up volumes before any destructive operation: `docker run --rm -v nexaops-postgres-data:/data -v $(pwd):/backup ubuntu tar czf /backup/postgres-backup.tar.gz /data`

